Deterministic vs probabilistic systems

Definition and Core Understanding
Deterministic Systems
A deterministic system is one where the outcome is fully determined by the initial conditions and the rules governing the system. Given the same input, the system will always produce the same output without any randomness.

Key property: No randomness or uncertainty.
Behavior: Predictable and reproducible.
Examples: Classical physics simulations, traditional algorithms, deterministic finite automata.
Probabilistic Systems
A probabilistic system incorporates randomness or uncertainty in its behavior. The same input can lead to different outputs, each with a certain probability.

Key property: Involves stochastic processes or randomness.
Behavior: Uncertain, outcomes described by probability distributions.
Examples: Weather forecasting models, Markov chains, Bayesian networks, Monte Carlo simulations.
Step-by-Step Explanation
Aspect	Deterministic System	Probabilistic System
Input-Output Relation	Fixed and predictable	Variable, probabilistic
Repeatability	Same input → Same output	Same input → Different outputs possible
Uncertainty	None	Present, modeled explicitly
Mathematical Model	Functions, differential equations	Probability distributions, stochastic processes
Use Cases	Algorithm design, control systems	Machine learning, AI, risk analysis
Example	Sorting algorithm (e.g., quicksort with fixed pivot)	Randomized algorithm, Monte Carlo method
